{"kind":"artifact","text":"The endpoint that carries the remote to the browser. Exact clean pushed head f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be on request/repo-remote-link; tree f08ef41f, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's current-basis request eb034253 and my promise 8abe2015.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. /v0/worktrees emits an optional remote beside the served path, and internal/service/ui.go serves the embedded browser that renders it. The endpoint is the reason the internal/app repair matters at this layer: a redirected read does not stay inside the process, it becomes a field in a response a person reads and clicks.\n\nWHAT THE REGRESSION AT THIS LAYER PROVES, separately from the app-layer one. worktrees_remote_test.go asserts at the response boundary that GIT_DIR, GIT_COMMON_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE cannot put an injected remote into the JSON the browser is handed. That is a different assertion from the app-layer test even though the same guard satisfies both: this one fails with the actual response body in the message, so a future change that reintroduced the redirection one layer up would be caught here by what a reader would have seen.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE. Reverting the config call site in internal/app fails TestWorktreesResponseIgnoresInheritedGitRoutingVariables/{GIT_DIR,GIT_COMMON_DIR} with the redirected body printed; reverting the rev-parse site fails its GIT_WORK_TREE subtest, where the injected value arrives as the repo path rather than the remote.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 229.171s, internal/kernel 157.364s, internal/app 157.137s, internal/docset 99.737s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt empty; build, vet and diff-check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"f6335179d5b8d0e908bf4cf0bf7211d7bd79d1be","path":"internal/service"}}